reviewers.summary:
Integrity review of the Spot path (full_guest.sh, spot_checkpoint.sh, and the launcher's checkpoint accounting). Nothing was run against the cloud. I ran test_spot_checkpoint.sh (41 cases, 0 failed) and one experiment on a made-up tree in my scratch directory, sweep.sh in work-integrity. The core chain holds:
- the state and manifest are pinned by checksum;
- every part's checksum is verified before it is extracted;
- tar members are confined to lib/ and ir/;
- attempt 1's baselines are compared before any restore or build;
- after the seal, the build user is locked and guarded before any compiled file arrives, and the restored lib manifest must equal the first seal's before C2 or C3 runs.

There are four integrity gaps:
1. The end check only covers the last machine. Results produced on earlier machines are never re-hashed after their checks ran, yet they are carried forward.
2. A sighting of the build user after the seal is uploaded only when a unit is recorded. It can be lost while that machine's replay results are kept.
3. Root's copy of the build user's archive is a check-then-copy race. The build user can swap in a symbolic link, and root then reads, hashes and uploads whatever the link points to.
4. The final sweep and the 'saved module changed' check look at the module name and two mtimes only. A rewritten or new file of a saved module is silently left out of the parts. I confirmed this by experiment. Every later attempt after the seal would then fail on the manifest comparison.

Smaller points: the C2 JSON files are not pinned, facts.json counts some figures per attempt, and the claim in CLAIMS.md about the rehearsed driver goes stale.
